1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,910
Orchard Core checks passwords and locks accounts with the options of ASP.NET Core Identity.

2
00:00:06,910 --> 00:00:11,724
In this video, you set the password requirements and the lockout rules in

3
00:00:11,724 --> 00:00:16,050
code and in configuration, and then see them at work on the site.

4
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:26,130
The Users module is built on ASP.NET Core Identity, whose IdentityOptions class holds

5
00:00:26,130 --> 00:00:29,202
the Password options and the Lockout options.

6
00:00:29,202 --> 00:00:33,659
Orchard Core does not set either of them, so the defaults of ASP.NET

7
00:00:33,659 --> 00:00:36,730
Core Identity apply until you configure them.

8
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:41,986
By default, a password needs at least six characters, with a digit, a

9
00:00:41,986 --> 00:00:46,613
lowercase letter, an uppercase letter and a non-alphanumeric character.

10
00:00:46,613 --> 00:00:51,900
Five failed attempts are allowed before an account is locked out for five minutes.

11
00:00:52,650 --> 00:00:56,833
The rules apply wherever a password is created, changed or reset.

12
00:00:56,833 --> 00:01:01,545
The Setup screen describes them, the Add User form can generate a matching

13
00:01:01,545 --> 00:01:05,080
password, and the sign-in form counts failed attempts.

14
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:13,124
In a module, a Startup class overrides ConfigureServices.

15
00:01:13,124 --> 00:01:19,227
It calls Configure for IdentityOptions, and sets the Password options, one line for each rule.

16
00:01:19,227 --> 00:01:21,302
These values are an example:

17
00:01:21,302 --> 00:01:24,780
choose values that meet your security requirements.

18
00:01:25,530 --> 00:01:29,448
Four Require options turn a character rule on or off:

19
00:01:29,448 --> 00:01:33,099
digit, lowercase, uppercase and non-alphanumeric.

20
00:01:33,099 --> 00:01:41,200
RequiredUniqueChars sets how many different characters a password needs, and RequiredLength sets its minimum length.

21
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:47,864
The Lockout options work the same way.

22
00:01:47,864 --> 00:01:53,352
MaxFailedAccessAttempts is the number of failed attempts allowed before an account is locked

23
00:01:53,352 --> 00:01:57,160
out, and DefaultLockoutTimeSpan is how long the lockout lasts.

24
00:01:57,910 --> 00:02:02,887
AllowedForNewUsers decides whether a newly created user can be locked out at all.

25
00:02:02,887 --> 00:02:06,720
The choice is saved on each user when the account is created.

26
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:15,210
Without a module of your own, set the same options in Program.cs, through

27
00:02:15,210 --> 00:02:18,631
the ConfigureServices method of the Orchard Core builder.

28
00:02:18,631 --> 00:02:20,260
It runs for each tenant.

29
00:02:21,010 --> 00:02:25,752
To change the values without building again, read them from configuration.

30
00:02:25,752 --> 00:02:32,510
Ask for IShellConfiguration in the Startup constructor, and bind its IdentityOptions section to the options.

31
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:38,930
The values then live in the OrchardCore section of appsettings.json, and environment variables

32
00:02:38,930 --> 00:02:40,461
can set them as well.

33
00:02:40,461 --> 00:02:45,280
Each tenant reads its own configuration, so a tenant can have rules of its own.

34
00:02:49,472 --> 00:02:52,038
Let's try the rules in the admin.

35
00:02:52,038 --> 00:02:55,919
Under Access Control, Users, Add User opens the form.

36
00:02:55,919 --> 00:03:01,972
We enter a user name, an email address, and a short password with no uppercase letter.

37
00:03:02,445 --> 00:03:06,516
Save refuses the password, and the page lists each rule it breaks:

38
00:03:06,516 --> 00:03:09,343
the minimum length and the uppercase letter.

39
00:03:09,343 --> 00:03:14,545
The digit and the non-alphanumeric character are not required, so they are not listed.

40
00:03:14,998 --> 00:03:21,755
The Generate password button fills both password fields with a password that follows the configured rules.

41
00:03:21,755 --> 00:03:24,048
Let's use it, and save the user.

42
00:03:28,252 --> 00:03:31,553
Let's sign in as that user with the wrong password.

43
00:03:31,553 --> 00:03:36,242
Each failed attempt is counted, and the page answers Invalid login attempt.

44
00:03:36,705 --> 00:03:39,672
The same answer comes back for each wrong password.

45
00:03:39,672 --> 00:03:43,681
Once the failed attempts pass the limit, the page changes its answer to

46
00:03:43,681 --> 00:03:47,795
The account is locked out, and the lockout lasts for the configured time.

47
00:03:49,409 --> 00:03:55,320
Signed in as an administrator, the Users list shows the locked user with a Locked badge.

48
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:58,339
The Actions menu of its row offers Unlock.

49
00:03:58,807 --> 00:04:04,121
Unlock asks for a confirmation, resets the failed attempt count and clears the lockout.

50
00:04:04,121 --> 00:04:06,007
The user can sign in again.

51
00:04:08,007 --> 00:04:12,615
ASP.NET Core Identity decides what a valid password is and when an account

52
00:04:12,615 --> 00:04:18,029
is locked, and the Users module follows the options you set in code or in configuration.

53
00:04:18,029 --> 00:04:19,527
Thanks for watching.

